---
import MetaPage from "@/components/MetaPage.astro"
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
---

<Layout>
  <MetaPage slot="head" />
  <prose-content class="legal-page">
    <h1>Privacy notice</h1>
    <p>Last updated: 23 October 2026</p>
    <h2>Controller</h2>
    <p>
      Bitcoin Austria – Verein zur Förderung von Bitcoin in Österreich<br>c/o
      STADLER PARTNER Rechtsanwälte GmbH<br>Seilerstätte 24, 1010 Vienna,
      Austria<br>Email:
      <a href="mailto:office@bitcoin-austria.at">office@bitcoin-austria.at</a>
      and <a href="mailto:kontakt@dac8.org">kontakt@dac8.org</a>
    </p>
    <h2>Summary</h2>
    <p>
      We do not set cookies or create user profiles. We measure page views in
      aggregate without recognising users across websites. We do not sell data
      and currently maintain no supporter list.
    </p>
    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      This website is served through <strong>Cloudflare</strong> (Cloudflare
      Inc., 101 Townsend St, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA). Accessing the
      website necessarily transmits connection data, such as your IP address,
      access time and browser type, to Cloudflare. This is required to deliver
      the website. Processing is based on Article 6(1)(f) GDPR, our legitimate
      interest in providing the website securely and efficiently. Connection
      data is stored only as long as needed to deliver and secure the website,
      for example to fend off attacks, and is deleted afterwards. Cloudflare is
      certified under the EU–US Data Privacy Framework. Further information:
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/"
        >cloudflare.com/privacypolicy</a
      >.
    </p>
    <h2>Web analytics</h2>
    <p>
      We use <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong>. This analytics tool does
      not set cookies or create user profiles. When you visit, your IP address
      is necessarily processed for a short time, for example to determine the
      country of origin, but it is not stored. We evaluate only aggregate access
      data, such as page views, country of origin and referrer, which does not
      allow conclusions about individuals. The legal basis is Article 6(1)(f)
      GDPR. Our legitimate interest is measuring the reach of our initiative.
    </p>
    <h2>Cookies and local preferences</h2>
    <p>
      We do not set cookies. We store your colour-scheme preference locally in
      your browser so it is kept for your next visit. This preference is not
      transmitted to us.
    </p>
    <h2>Letter to members of parliament</h2>
    <p>
      The letter on the get-involved page is drafted entirely in your browser.
      What you type into the fields is not transmitted to us and not stored. You
      send the letter yourself, for example through your email program; the
      terms of your email provider and the parliament apply to that
      transmission.
    </p>
    <h2>Contact by email</h2>
    <p>
      If you contact us by email, we process your message and the data it
      contains, such as your name, email address and message content, to handle
      your enquiry. The legal basis is Article 6(1)(f) GDPR, or Article 6(1)(b)
      for contract-related enquiries. Our legitimate interest is answering
      enquiries and communicating with people who contact us about this
      initiative. We keep your emails as long as they are needed to handle the
      enquiry and for further correspondence with you. We delete them on
      request, unless statutory retention obligations require otherwise.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our email service uses <strong>Google Workspace</strong> (Google Ireland
      Ltd., Gordon House, Barrow Street, Dublin 4, Ireland). Google is certified
      under the EU–US Data Privacy Framework. Emails are processed and stored on
      Google’s servers. Further information:
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy"
        >policies.google.com/privacy</a
      >.
    </p>
    <h2>Registration and supporters</h2>
    <p>
      The initiative on dac8.org currently has no registration, petition list or
      newsletter of its own. We do not currently collect supporters’ names,
      postal addresses or powers of attorney. If a procedural step requires such
      information, we will collect it for that specific purpose, explain this at
      collection and update this notice. Our RSS feed requires no registration.
      For the association’s newsletter, we link to Bitcoin Austria’s website,
      where registration takes place.
    </p>
    <h2>Donations</h2>
    <p>
      Bitcoin donations are handled through our own BTCPay server. The donation
      page requests neither a name nor an email address. Technically necessary
      connection data is processed when it is accessed. BTCPay maintains invoice
      and payment data needed for processing, such as amount, status, payment
      address and transaction reference. We process this data to handle and
      record donations and meet statutory retention and accounting obligations,
      based on Article 6(1)(f) or Article 6(1)(c) GDPR.
    </p>
    <p>
      For bank-transfer donations, we receive the data transmitted with the
      transfer, such as name, IBAN and payment reference, and process it to
      handle and record the donation. The legal basis is Article 6(1)(f) GDPR
      and Article 6(1)(c) for statutory retention and accounting obligations.
    </p>
    <p>
      For both ways of donating, our legitimate interest is handling and
      recording donations properly. We keep the data as long as needed to
      process the donation and as long as statutory retention obligations apply,
      in particular under tax and association law, and delete it afterwards.
    </p>
    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You have rights of access, rectification, erasure, restriction of
      processing, data portability and objection. Contact
      office@bitcoin-austria.at with any concerns. Complaints may be submitted
      to the Austrian Data Protection Authority (dsb.gv.at).
    </p>
    <p>
      Where we process your data on the basis of Article 6(1)(f) GDPR, you have
      the right under Article 21 GDPR to object to this processing at any time
      on grounds relating to your particular situation.
    </p>
    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      We update this privacy notice as needed. The current version applies from
      the time it is published on this website.
    </p>
  </prose-content>
</Layout>
